    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p class="lt-chem-138794">Although the variation in the results appears to be greater for the second 10 samples, the results do not violate any of the six rules. There is no evidence in <a title="15.4: Evaluating Quality Assurance Data" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/15%3A_Quality_Assurance/15.04%3A_Evaluating_Quality_Assurance_Data#figure15.4.3" rel="internal">Figure 15.4.3
</a> that the analysis is out of statistical control. The next three results, in which two of the three results are between the <em>UWL </em>and the <em>UCL</em>, violates the second rule. Because the analysis is no longer under statistical control, we must stop using the glucometer until we determine the source of the problem.</p>
    </dd>
